VERSIONS (REAL published npm packages, installed at build time; no source edits)
  V0 vulnerable  : @adobe/css-tools@4.3.0  (pre CVE-2023-26364; @import regex exponential + selector `|\(.*?\)`)
  V1 incomplete  : @adobe/css-tools@4.3.1  (CVE-2023-26364 fix = TARGET; @import `(?:;|$)` terminator added,
                                            BUT selector() `|\(.*?\)` branch UNCHANGED -> residual)
  V2 fixed       : @adobe/css-tools@4.3.2  (CVE-2023-48631 fix; selector `|\(.*?\)` branch removed,
                                            parens handled by an explicit non-regex findClosingParenthese scan)
Build confirmation (docker build log):
  installed @adobe/css-tools 4.3.0 parse=function
  installed @adobe/css-tools 4.3.1 parse=function
  installed @adobe/css-tools 4.3.2 parse=function

TWO DISTINCT CATASTROPHIC REGEXES (sibling-regex incomplete fix)
  original (CVE-2023-26364) : the `@import` at-rule regex in _compileAtrule. V0 `^@import\s*((:?[^;'"]|...)+);`
                              backtracks exponentially on a colon-only run with no `;`. V1 adds a
                              `(?:;|$)` end-alternation -> the @import bomb is CLOSED on V1.
  residual (CVE-2023-48631) : the selector() comma-split regex `/("|')(?:\1|.)*?\1|\(.*?\)/g` — IDENTICAL
                              on V0 AND V1 (CVE-2023-26364 never touched it). The `|\(.*?\)` branch on a
                              long unmatched-'(' run (when the selector has a comma) is O(n^2). V2 removes
                              the branch -> residual CLOSED on V2. (residual therefore fires on V0 too;
                              the gate differential is original-blocked-on-V1 + residual-slow-on-V1.)

ARCHITECTURE (web-real trigger)
  Dockerfile         : node:18-slim, `npm i express@4 @adobe/css-tools@${CSS_TOOLS_VERSION}`
  app/server.js      : Express service
                         GET  /health        -> {ok, css_tools_version}
                         POST /parse {css}    -> const ast = css.parse(req.body.css, {silent:true});
                                                 res.json({ok, rules, server_ms, len})
                       css.parse() runs SYNCHRONOUSLY on the Node event loop, so a catastrophic input
                       hangs the HTTP response = server-side ReDoS observable as external latency.
                       (server_ms in the JSON body = the in-process parse time, confirming the latency
                        is genuine parse compute, not network.)
  docker-compose.yml : profiles v0/v1/v2, ports 26364/26365/26366, cpus=1.0, mem 512m, stop_grace 2s.
  oracle.py          : per profile, per payload -> kill + `up -d` a FRESH server (a synchronous parse
                       permanently wedges Node's single event loop, so each probe needs a clean server),
                       wait /health, POST /parse from OUTSIDE the container, measure the client
                       round-trip latency (10s read timeout => hang recorded as ~10000ms).

PAYLOADS (bounded length; blow-up is pure-CPU backtracking, flat memory)
  original (CVE-2023-26364) : '@import ' + ':'x40             (len 48)     colon-only @import, no ';' terminator
  residual (CVE-2023-48631) : 'a,' + '('x140000 + '{}'        (len 140004) comma forces selector() split; O(n^2) paren scan
  benign   (control)        : well-formed CSS (comma selectors, quoted attr w/ commas, balanced parens, @import)  (len 133)

